From: Igni <igni.li@quectel.com>
Subject: [PATCH] pw-pal-plugin: hold one PAL handle per direction, PAL hands out one graph instance per process

PAL does not pick the ACDB graph instance from the device. For every stream
that is not voice-UI, non-tunnel or sensor-PCM it is the first free bit of a
per-direction bitmask in the process -- ResourceManager::getStreamInstanceID()
sets stream_instances[PAL_STREAM_DEEP_BUFFER - 1] for output and
in_stream_instances[] for input, and frees the bit when the stream is
destroyed. The ACDB tables this board ships only carry Instance_1 for the deep
buffer graphs, so the second deep-buffer stream of a direction can never
start: gsl_get_graph_alias returns -ENODEV, graph_open exits -19, and the sink
stays silent while its node, its port and its entry in the desktop picker all
still look healthy.

Two of the six instances reaching STREAMING together is enough, and a single
jack event does it: plugging the headset moves the default input to the
headset mic while the speaker-mic stream is still open. Measured at 16:39:25
-- the headset mic gets "Instance: 2", graph_open exits -19, and the
speaker-mic handle closes 100 ms later, so the retry would have gone straight
in. What is left behind is worse than the one failed open: PAL had already
switched a stream onto the headset output during that open, so device id 4
stays at deviceCount 1 for the rest of the boot and every later headset stream
asks for Instance_2 again. That is the "no sound, but everything looks
connected" state which only a pipewire restart cleared, and why pulling and
re-inserting the jack a few times is enough to kill a channel for good.

Keep one handle per direction in the process and let the other instance wait
on the existing 2 s retry instead of opening a handle that cannot work: the
bit is freed by whoever holds it as soon as that stream closes, and every
teardown path closes (PAUSED, hidden node, module destroy, failed open). The
six instances share one process, so the registry is a file-static array of the
live userdata, registered next to the module listener and dropped again in
pw_pal_userdata_destroy().

Checked: series 0001-0007 applies in bitbake's order and the patched source
compiles with 0 errors.

Signed-off-by: Igni <igni.li@quectel.com>
---
--- a/pw-pal-plugin.c
+++ b/pw-pal-plugin.c
@@ -335,6 +335,75 @@
                 udata->retry_timer, &zero, &zero, false);
 }
 
+/*
+ * One deep-buffer handle per direction, process-wide.
+ *
+ * PAL does not take the ACDB graph instance from the device: for everything
+ * that is not voice-UI / non-tunnel / sensor-PCM it is the first free bit of a
+ * per-direction, per-stream-type bitmask held by the process
+ * (ResourceManager::getStreamInstanceID(), stream_instances[PAL_STREAM_DEEP_BUFFER - 1]
+ * for output and in_stream_instances[] for input), released again when the
+ * stream is destroyed. The ACDB tables only carry Instance_1 for the deep
+ * buffer graphs this board uses, so the second deep-buffer stream in the
+ * process asks for a graph that does not exist: gsl_get_graph_alias answers
+ * -ENODEV (-19), the stream never starts, and the module keeps its node, its
+ * port and its place in the desktop picker -- the sink looks healthy and is
+ * silent, and restarting pipewire is the only way back.
+ *
+ * Two of the six instances reaching STREAMING at once is all it takes, and a
+ * jack event does that by itself: plugging the headset moves the default input
+ * to the headset mic while the speaker-mic stream is still open (measured:
+ * "Instance: 2" on the headset mic, graph_open -19, the speaker-mic handle
+ * closing 100 ms later -- the retry would have been in).
+ *
+ * So hold one handle per direction and let the other instance wait instead of
+ * opening a doomed one every time: the slot frees as soon as the current owner
+ * closes, which every teardown path does (PAUSED, hidden node, module destroy,
+ * failed open).
+ */
+#define PAL_MAX_INSTANCES       16
+
+static struct pw_userdata *pal_instances[PAL_MAX_INSTANCES];
+
+static void pw_pal_register_instance(struct pw_userdata *udata)
+{
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < PAL_MAX_INSTANCES; i++) {
+        if (pal_instances[i] == NULL) {
+            pal_instances[i] = udata;
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+    pw_log_error("%s: no free instance slot", udata->jack_name);
+}
+
+static void pw_pal_unregister_instance(struct pw_userdata *udata)
+{
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < PAL_MAX_INSTANCES; i++)
+        if (pal_instances[i] == udata)
+            pal_instances[i] = NULL;
+}
+
+static int pw_pal_instance_busy(struct pw_userdata *udata)
+{
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < PAL_MAX_INSTANCES; i++) {
+        struct pw_userdata *other = pal_instances[i];
+
+        if (other == NULL || other == udata)
+            continue;
+        if (other->isplayback != udata->isplayback)
+            continue;
+        if (other->stream_handle != NULL)
+            return -EAGAIN;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
 static int pw_pal_stream_open_start(struct pw_userdata *udata)
 {
     int rc = 0;
@@ -345,6 +414,14 @@
         pw_log_error("no audio path yet (%d)", rc);
         return rc;      /* pw_pal_stream_start() arms the retry timer on this */
     }
+
+    rc = pw_pal_instance_busy(udata);
+    if (rc) {
+        pw_log_info("%s: another %s stream holds the ACDB instance, waiting",
+                udata->jack_name, udata->isplayback ? "playback" : "capture");
+        return rc;      /* retryable: the owner frees the bit when it closes */
+    }
+
     rc = pal_stream_open(udata->stream_attributes, 1, udata->pal_device, 0, NULL, pa_pal_out_cb, udata,
             &udata->stream_handle);
 
@@ -718,6 +795,7 @@
     pw_properties_free(udata->stream_props);
     pw_properties_free(udata->props);
 
+    pw_pal_unregister_instance(udata);
     free(udata);
 }
 
@@ -1425,6 +1503,7 @@
     /* Before anything can fail into pw_impl_module_schedule_destroy(): the
      * module's .destroy is what runs pw_pal_userdata_destroy(). */
     pw_impl_module_add_listener(module, &udata->module_listener, &pw_pal_events_module, udata);
+    pw_pal_register_instance(udata);
     if (udata->jack_name[0] != '\0') {
         /*
          * A jacked instance gets its stream from the jack state instead of
